{"id":"a869999b-e462-4c4e-a98c-ef0c2b55b037","object":"chat.completion","created":1785009806,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must be a dict\\\")\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"nodes and required_symbols must be lists\\\")\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed = []\\n    # process deeper first, tiebreak by id ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        # never remove root\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        # must be removable\\n        if not node.get('removable', False):\\n            continue\\n        # cannot remove a required symbol node\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        # cannot remove if it has any retained child\\n        has_retained_child = False\\n        for child_id in children.get(nid, []):\\n            if child_id in retained:\\n                has_retained_child = True\\n                break\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        # check that all required symbols remain after removal\\n        # removing this node also implies its subtree is removed, but because we process deeper first,\\n        # any retained descendant would have prevented this node from being considered.\\n        # Still, we do a full check for safety.\\n        symbols_after = set()\\n        for rid in retained:\\n            if rid != nid:\\n                symbols_after.add(node_map[rid]['symbol'])\\n        if not all(sym in symbols_after for sym in required_symbols):\\n            continue\\n        # removal is allowed\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n    # retained list sorted by id\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted\\n    }\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function `solve(case)` that implements grammar-guided node reduction based on the described algorithm. The task is SE-PE-01. The input case has fields: nodes (list of node objects with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols (list). The root node has parent null. We need to return an object with removed_node_ids (ordered list of removed node IDs) and retained_node_ids (sorted list of retained node IDs). The algorithm: deeper nodes first (higher depth, so we need to process in descending depth order). For ties at same depth, use node ID as stable tiebreak. Root never removed. Nodes that are not removable cannot be removed. Nodes with required symbol cannot be removed? Actually, \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So required symbol nodes cannot be removed either. Also, \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So after tentative removal, we must check that all required symbols still appear in the remaining tree (including ancestors and descendants). Also, we cannot remove a node if it has children that are retained (i.e., if any child node is not removed). So we process nodes in order; when considering a node, we check if it is removable, not required symbol, not root, and if all required symbols still present if we remove it, and that it has no children that are not yet removed. Since we process deeper nodes first, children (which have higher depth) will be processed before their parent. So when we consider a parent, its descendants (children, grandchildren etc.) that are removable might have been removed already. But we need to ensure that if any child node is retained (i.e., not removed), then parent cannot be removed. That means we need to know which nodes are retained after processing deeper nodes. Also required symbols: we need to verify that all required symbols are present in the set of retained nodes (including ancestors, descendants) after removal. But we process node by node; we could simulate removal and check if all required symbols still exist.\n\nImplementation approach: Build a mapping from node ID to node object. Also build adjacency: parent to children list. We need to track which nodes are removed. We'll process nodes sorted by depth descending, then by ID ascending (since tie break by ID as stable). For each node in that order, if not removable, skip. If it is root, skip. If its symbol is in required_symbols, skip. Then check if any of its children (direct children) are still retained. Since we process children first (deeper), children are either removed or retained. We can have a set `retained` initially all node IDs. Then as we remove nodes, we remove from retained. But we also need to consider that a node may have descendants that are removed but not children? Actually, if a node has any child that is retained, then the node cannot be removed. So we need to check immediate children only? The description says \"a node with retained children\". That suggests immediate children? Usually in tree reduction, a node cannot be removed if it has any child that is not removed. Because if you remove a node, you either remove its subtree or replace it? But the problem says \"replacement candidates\" but in this implementation it seems just deletion? The instructions \"Tentatively remove a node\" suggests we are removing nodes. Also \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" means if a node has children that are not removed, we cannot remove it because that would orphan them. So we check direct children. But what about grandchildren? If a child is removed, its subtree is gone, so that's fine. So we check if any child is still in retained set.\n\nNow the required_symbols check: After tentative removal (i.e., if we remove this node), would all required symbols be present in the tree (including root)? We need to check that for each required symbol, there exists at least one node that is not removed and has that symbol. Since we are iterating, we can compute a set of symbols of retained nodes. Before removal, we have current set of symbols from retained nodes. If we remove a node, it loses its symbol. So we need to ensure that after removing, each required symbol still appears at least once. So we can check if the symbol of the node being removed is required? But we already skip required symbol nodes. So the node's symbol is not required. However, removing a node might also remove its entire subtree? Wait, if we remove a node, we are not removing its descendants? The algorithm says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That implies that removing a node removes everything below it? Or just the node? In parse tree pruning, removing a node often removes its subtree. But the instructions also say \"never remove ... a node with retained children\". That suggests children are separate; if we remove a node, its children are also removed? But then they wouldn't be retained children. Actually, \"retained children\" means children that are not removed. So if we remove a node, all its children and descendants are also removed? That would contradict processing order. Usually in such reduction algorithms, when you remove a node, you also remove its entire subtree because the node is the root of that subtree. So the children would be removed implicitly. But then the condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" is because you cannot have children that are retained after removing the parent. So if you remove a parent, all its children must also be removed (or they become orphans). But the algorithm processes deeper nodes first, so children might be removed before parent. That means if a child is removed, it's fine to remove the parent (since no retained children). If a child is not removed (e.g., because it is required or not removable), then parent cannot be removed. That makes sense.\n\nThus, when we remove a node, we should also remove all its descendants? But the problem says \"Tentatively remove a node\" and we need to check if required symbols remain. If we only remove that node, the descendants remain, but then they are orphaned (no parent). That seems inconsistent. However, the condition \"never remove ... a node with retained children\" implies that if we remove a node, we must also remove its children. So effectively, removing a node means removing the entire subtree rooted at that node. The algorithm then processes deeper nodes first; so if a node is removable and its children (descendants) are removed earlier, then removing the node just adds to removal list. But if a child is retained, we cannot remove the node because that child would be lost? Actually, if child is retained, we cannot remove the node because removal would also remove the child (since subtree). That matches \"never remove a node with retained children\". So yes, removal of a node implies removal of its entire subtree. But that contradicts \"deeper nodes before shallower nodes\" because if we remove a node, all its descendants are removed automatically. So the order of processing deeper nodes first is to decide which nodes to remove individually; but if a parent is removed, all its children (and further) are removed regardless. So we need to be careful: We cannot remove a node if any of its descendants are required or not removable? Actually, the algorithm should allow removal of a parent only if all its descendants have been removed (or are also removable and can be removed). The process: We iterate nodes in descending depth order. For each node, we try to remove it (subject to conditions). If we remove it, we add it to removed list, and we also remove its entire subtree from the retained set? But then we might double-count children that were already removed. However, if we have already processed a child and removed it, that child is already marked removed. So when we process parent, we can simply check if any child is still retained (i.e., not removed). If yes, we cannot remove parent. If all children are already removed, then removing parent is allowed (provided other conditions). This matches the process: deeper nodes processed first; we decide whether to remove them individually. If we remove a child, its parent later may become removable if all its children are gone. But note: if a child is removable and we remove it, its subtree (grandchildren) are also removed (since we would have processed them earlier). That's fine.\n\nThus, we need to maintain a set `retained` of nodes that are currently not removed. Initially all nodes. We process nodes in descending depth order, and for each node, we check conditions. If we decide to remove it, we remove it from retained (and also all its descendants? Actually, if we processed children first, they might already be removed. But we need to ensure that when we remove a node, its descendants are also removed from retained, in case some descendants were not processed yet? But we are processing in descending depth order, so all descendants (higher depth) are processed before the node. So they have already been considered. If they were removable, they might have been removed. If they were not removable, they remain retained. So when we process the node, if any of its immediate children are retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove the node. That is correct. There's no need to remove descendants because